1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,690
Look down at your phone.

2
00:00:01,690 --> 00:00:08,109
It feels like magic, but inside, it is fundamentally just a handful of sand from the beach.

3
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:11,779
Sand is made of tiny quartz crystals called silicon.

4
00:00:11,779 --> 00:00:20,949
Humans figured out that if you melt sand down and carve billions of microscopic switches into it, you can trap electricity inside.

5
00:00:20,949 --> 00:00:23,480
When a switch is OFF, it means ZERO.

6
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:25,670
When it flips ON, it means ONE.

7
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:27,500
That is all a computer is:

8
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:30,960
a beach of sand with lightning flipping switches.

9
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:33,990
So how on earth did we teach sand to think?

10
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:39,350
In 1950, a British math genius named Alan Turing published a landmark paper.

11
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,329
He asked seven simple words:

12
00:00:41,329 --> 00:00:44,000
"Can machines think?"

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,009
Turing imagined a machine with an infinite paper tape, like a never-ending comic strip.

14
00:00:49,009 --> 00:00:57,829
And he proved that every computer in the universe only needs seven simple rules—what mathematicians call the seven variables:

15
00:00:57,829 --> 00:00:58,460
First, Q:

16
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:00,789
the robot's mood or memory state.

17
00:01:00,789 --> 00:01:01,780
Second, Sigma:

18
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:05,299
the secret alphabet it reads, like zeros and ones.

19
00:01:05,299 --> 00:01:06,219
Third, Gamma:

20
00:01:06,219 --> 00:01:09,180
all the symbols written on its paper tape.

21
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:10,170
Fourth, Delta:

22
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:11,299
the recipe book.

23
00:01:11,299 --> 00:01:15,319
If you see a one, erase it, write a zero, and step right!

24
00:01:15,319 --> 00:01:16,310
Fifth, Q-zero:

25
00:01:16,310 --> 00:01:19,269
the start button where the robot wakes up.

26
00:01:19,269 --> 00:01:19,900
Sixth, B:

27
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:23,569
an empty blank box on the paper waiting for an idea.

28
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:24,629
And seventh, F:

29
00:01:24,629 --> 00:01:29,000
the finish line, when the puzzle is solved and the robot says:

30
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:29,629
I did it!

31
00:01:29,629 --> 00:01:38,590
Six years later, in 1956, two scientists named John McCarthy and Claude Shannon gathered in an attic room at Dartmouth College.

32
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:42,049
They officially gave this dream a brand-new name:

33
00:01:42,049 --> 00:01:43,879
"Artificial Intelligence."

34
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:46,599
Early computers were clumsy.

35
00:01:46,599 --> 00:01:51,599
A top machine in the 1960s had less memory than a modern kitchen toaster!

36
00:01:51,599 --> 00:02:06,760
When scientists asked a computer to translate the Russian saying, "The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak"—meaning you want to do your chores, but your body is too sleepy—the computer had no idea what feelings were.

37
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:08,129
It translated it as:

38
00:02:08,129 --> 00:02:12,300
"The vodka is good, but the meat is rotten!"

39
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:21,289
Then, in 1969, a brilliant scientist named Marvin Minsky proved that early artificial brains couldn't even solve a basic child's puzzle called XOR.

40
00:02:21,289 --> 00:02:23,479
Imagine four cookies on a table:

41
00:02:23,479 --> 00:02:25,270
A cookie with plain dough?

42
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:25,469
No.

43
00:02:25,469 --> 00:02:28,080
A cookie with chocolate and sprinkles?

44
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:28,280
No.

45
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:30,270
A cookie with ONLY chocolate?

46
00:02:30,270 --> 00:02:30,539
Yes!

47
00:02:30,539 --> 00:02:32,530
A cookie with ONLY sprinkles?

48
00:02:32,530 --> 00:02:32,810
Yes!

49
00:02:32,810 --> 00:02:40,280
Can you place one single straight ruler on the table to separate the two YES cookies from the two NO cookies?

50
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:40,759
Try it.

51
00:02:40,759 --> 00:02:42,680
It is physically impossible!

52
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:47,750
A straight line cannot separate cookies that sit diagonal from each other.

53
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:52,000
Because early AI could only draw one straight line, it failed.

54
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,900
In 1973, a British report by Sir James Lighthill concluded AI had failed its promises.

55
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:03,039
Governments pulled funding, and the world entered the cold First AI Winter.

56
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:09,530
While the world gave up, a stubborn researcher named Geoffrey Hinton refused to quit.

57
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:12,680
Hinton believed neural networks weren't broken.

58
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:14,560
They just needed two things:

59
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:21,319
millions of real photos, and chips that could do thousands of math problems at the exact same second.

60
00:03:21,319 --> 00:03:23,860
And then came an accidental discovery:

61
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:24,669
video games!

62
00:03:24,669 --> 00:03:32,240
To draw a 3D video game on your screen, a graphics card has to color two million pixels sixty times every second.

63
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:39,069
A normal computer processor is like a brilliant professor who solves one giant math problem at a time.

64
00:03:39,069 --> 00:03:47,240
A gaming graphics card is like an army of thousands of painters who can all multiply colors at the exact same millisecond.

65
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:54,069
And coloring video game pixels turns out to be the exact same math as training artificial brain cells!

66
00:03:54,069 --> 00:04:03,180
In October 2012, Hinton and his student Ilya Sutskever created an artificial brain called AlexNet, powered by two gaming graphics cards.

67
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:13,830
At a world contest identifying a million images, AlexNet destroyed every standard computer, dropping the error rate from twenty-six percent to fifteen percent.

68
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:16,839
The modern deep learning revolution was born.

69
00:04:17,639 --> 00:04:22,129
Even with graphics cards, computers still read like anxious robots:

70
00:04:22,129 --> 00:04:22,589
word...

71
00:04:22,589 --> 00:04:22,930
by...

72
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:23,259
word.

73
00:04:23,259 --> 00:04:28,550
By the time they reached the end of a long sentence, they forgot the beginning!

74
00:04:28,550 --> 00:04:33,579
Then, in 2017, eight researchers at Google published a revolutionary paper:

75
00:04:33,579 --> 00:04:35,600
"Attention Is All You Need."

76
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,529
They invented the Transformer.

77
00:04:37,529 --> 00:04:44,360
Instead of reading one word at a time, a transformer looks at every word in a sentence simultaneously.

78
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:45,759
Imagine the sentence:

79
00:04:45,759 --> 00:04:49,399
"The robot didn't cross the street because IT was too tired."

80
00:04:49,399 --> 00:04:50,850
Who was tired?

81
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:51,589
The street?

82
00:04:51,589 --> 00:04:52,459
Or the robot?

83
00:04:52,459 --> 00:04:55,810
A human child instantly knows the robot was tired.

84
00:04:55,810 --> 00:05:02,569
The transformer figures this out by shining invisible mathematical laser beams between all the words.

85
00:05:02,569 --> 00:05:06,800
The word "IT" shines its brightest laser beam directly at "ROBOT."

86
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,540
That laser connection is called an Attention Weight.

87
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:22,120
Scale that attention equation up with supercomputers, and you get modern language models like ChatGPT and Gemini that can write poems, explain homework, and understand code.

88
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:26,819
For seventy years, AI only analyzed what humans gave it.

89
00:05:26,819 --> 00:05:29,540
It tagged photos and predicted numbers.

90
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:34,480
Then in 2020, scientists taught computers how to dream using diffusion.

91
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:42,910
Imagine taking a crystal-clear photograph and slowly sprinkling static noise over it until it looks like television snow.

92
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:53,980
Scientists taught a neural network how to reverse that snow step by step—wiping away the noise until a brand new, photorealistic image appears out of thin air.

93
00:05:53,980 --> 00:05:59,620
By 2024, diffusion merged with transformers to create video generators like Sora.

94
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:02,689
Computers weren't just reading text anymore.

95
00:06:02,689 --> 00:06:08,399
They were imagining 3D worlds, simulating reflections, water ripples, and gravity.

96
00:06:09,199 --> 00:06:11,279
And that brings us to today.

97
00:06:11,279 --> 00:06:13,649
A chatbot is just a talking toy:

98
00:06:13,649 --> 00:06:16,399
you type a prompt, it gives you text.

99
00:06:16,399 --> 00:06:19,300
The real frontier is Autonomous Agents:

100
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:22,639
artificial intelligence with hands and tools.

101
00:06:22,639 --> 00:06:27,000
An agent is given a big goal—like "create a full animated video."

102
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,029
Instead of guessing, it acts like a smart team:

103
00:06:31,029 --> 00:06:32,959
First, it plans the steps.

104
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:35,259
Second, it uses external tools:

105
00:06:35,259 --> 00:06:39,339
running terminals, editing files, and drawing graphics.

106
00:06:39,339 --> 00:06:46,910
Third, if it makes a mistake, it reads the error, debugs its own code, and tries again until it works!

107
00:06:46,910 --> 00:06:58,569
From Alan Turing's paper tape in 1950 to autonomous agents directing complete documentaries in 2026, the story of AI has never been about replacing humanity.

108
00:06:58,569 --> 00:07:02,199
It has always been our grandest scientific quest:

109
00:07:02,199 --> 00:07:07,920
using sand and lightning to understand the miracle of how our own minds work.
